\begin_layout Section
Literature Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This paper relates most directly to the literature distinguishing opportunity from necessity entrepreneurship.
 Much of the empirical literature operationalizes this distinction using observable proxies,
 especially stated motives or pre-entry labor-market status.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "fairlie_fossen_2019"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 propose an objective classification based on pre-entry work status and show that opportunity and necessity entry display different cyclical patterns.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "fairlie_2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "fossen_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 likewise show that entrepreneurial entry is heterogeneous over the cycle,
 with unemployment-driven and lower-scale entry behaving differently from more growth-oriented business creation.
 Using GEM microdata,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chivers_2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show that necessity entrepreneurship is negatively associated with economic development,
 whereas opportunity entrepreneurship is positively associated with development.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "poschke_2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 provide the closest bridge from these empirical patterns to a structural interpretation by emphasizing how labor-market prospects shape entrepreneurial selection,
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "levine_rubinstein_2018"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 further show that entrepreneurship and other forms of self-employment should not be treated as interchangeable objects.
 This literature makes clear that entrepreneurial entry is heterogeneous,
 but it usually measures that heterogeneity through reduced-form proxies rather than through the occupational-choice problem itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper also builds on the occupational-choice tradition in macroeconomics and entrepreneurship.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lucas_1978"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "evans_jovanovic_1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "buera_2009"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show why entrepreneurial selection,
 firm scale,
 and financial constraints should be studied jointly rather than through entry alone.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "hurst_pugsley_2011"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 add an important empirical qualification:
 much small-business activity is not well described by the standard high-growth view of entrepreneurship.
 That point is central here.
 If weaker outside options raise primarily low-scale self-employment rather than employer entrepreneurship,
 then the relevant outcome is not simply whether business ownership rises,
 but which type of business activity expands and with what implications for firm scale,
 output,
 and welfare.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper also relates to a smaller but growing literature that embeds self-employment in equilibrium search environments.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mortensen_pissarides_1994"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "petrongolo_pissarides_2001"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 provide the benchmark search-and-matching framework for job-finding rates,
 separations,
 and labor-market tightness.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "albrecht_navarro_vroman_2009"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "satchi_temple_2009"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show that in economies with formal and informal sectors,
 search frictions shape the allocation of workers across unemployment,
 wage work,
 and self-employment.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "narita_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "denderski_sniekers_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "poschke_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 move closer to the present paper by modeling self-employment as an alternative to job search and showing that changes in labor-market frictions materially affect self-employment outcomes.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chodorow_reich_karabarbounis_2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 add that the opportunity cost of employment is itself cyclical,
 while 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "hombert_schoar_sraer_thesmar_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show that unemployment insurance can raise entrepreneurial entry by reducing downside risk.
 The present paper brings these strands together in a setting where job-finding risk,
 separation risk,
 and unemployment insurance jointly shift the occupational-choice threshold,
 making the distinction between opportunity and necessity entrepreneurship endogenous to labor-market conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper also relates to the literature on entrepreneurial composition over the business cycle.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "sedlacek_sterk_2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show that startup employment is procyclical and that cohort differences reflect startup size and growth potential,
 not only startup counts.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "haltiwanger_jarmin_miranda_2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "fort_etal_2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 similarly show that young firms,
 startup job creation,
 and firm scale respond strongly to cyclical conditions.
 This evidence suggests that changes in entrepreneurship should not be interpreted through a single extensive-margin statistic.
 That perspective aligns closely with the central prediction of this paper:
 weaker outside options can increase lower-scale business ownership even when employer-oriented entry or startup growth potential weakens.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Relative to these literatures,
 the paper contributes in two ways.
 First,
 it offers a structural definition of necessity entrepreneurship grounded in optimal occupational choice rather than in motives or prior status alone.
 Second,
 it embeds that distinction in a framework with heterogeneous human capital,
 entrepreneurial ability,
 search frictions,
 closure risk,
 and social insurance,
 allowing the model to speak not only to entrepreneurial entry,
 but also to entrepreneurial composition,
 firm scale,
 and the distinction between self-employment and employer entrepreneurship.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Model:
 Economic Environment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a Lucas (1978) span of control model,
 where individuals differ in the level of human capital,
 
\begin_inset Formula $h.$
\end_inset

 As in Gomez and Kuhen (2017),
 individuals are educated to either the primary 
\begin_inset Formula $(P)$
\end_inset

,
 secondary 
\begin_inset Formula $(S)$
\end_inset

 or tertiary level 
\begin_inset Formula $(T)$
\end_inset

.
 The level of education means that a tertiary educated individuals have a higher draw of human capital,
 on average,
 than secondary educated individuals,
 who in turn have a higher draw on average than primary educated individuals.
 This human capital draw is positively correlated with managerial ability 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 as well as labor productivity 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the start of the period agents enter as either unemployed 
\begin_inset Formula $($
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $i_{w}=0)$
\end_inset

,
 employed 
\begin_inset Formula $($
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $i_{w}=1)$
\end_inset

,
 or an entrepreneur 
\begin_inset Formula $($
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $i_{w}=2)$
\end_inset

.
 Individuals face a choice of whether to become an entrepreneur,
 to continue working or to search for work.
 However,
 the value of employment is different depending on whether an agent enters the period as employed 
\begin_inset Formula $($
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $i_{w}=1)$
\end_inset

 or not.
 Although there is a risk that an employee may become separated from a firm with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

,
 this is less risky than searching for work which has a 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\theta)$
\end_inset

 probability of obtaining employment.
 Consequently,
 the expected value of employment is higher for those already in employment than those who are not.
 Additionally,
 the probability of separation is lower for individuals with higher levels of human capital and they are able to command a higher wage due to higher levels of productivity.
 So the value of employment,
 holding other things equal,
 is higher for those with higher human capital as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The innovation of this model is that the entrepreneurial decision results in two types of entrepreneurs emerge,
 opportunity-based entrepreneurs and necessity-based entrepreneurs.
 We define opportunity-based entrepreneurs are those whose value of entrepreneurship is higher,
 even if they had employment.
 Necessity-based entrepreneurs are those who would not have become entrepreneurs,
 if they had employment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Timing:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The timing of events can be summarized below.
 Occupational choice happens at the start of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t.$
\end_inset

 Those choosing to become entrepreneurs run projects and choose capital and hired labor each period.
 If hired labor satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}=0$
\end_inset

 the business is nonemployer self-employment,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}>0$
\end_inset

 corresponds to employer entrepreneurship.
 Entrepreneurial businesses face closure risk 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{e}(h)$
\end_inset

 .
 In the benchmark,
 closure moves the household out of business and into non-employment without automatic regular unemployment insurance eligibility.
 Those who were previously employed within a project can decide to carry on working but carry a risk of separation with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

.
 If separation occurs these workers will claim unemployed benefits and be unable to look for work until the following period.
 After separation occurs,
 vacancies are posted and who were unemployed in the previous period are matched with firms.
 Those that are successful and match with with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\theta)$
\end_inset

 go on to earn a wage.
 Whereas those that are unsuccessful will claim unemployment benefits and be unable to search again until the following period.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike the search and matching literature there is no wage bargaining between workers and the firm at the end of matching.
 Instead the wage pins down the occupational choice decision which determines those wanting to become entrepreneurs and those who will search for work.
 Crucially,
 the worker only finds out whether they enter production and earn a wage after their occupational choice decision.
\end_layout

