\begin_layout Paragraph
Production:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Firms use labor 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 and capital 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 to produce a single consumption good,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 Capital depreciates at a constant rate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

.Managers can operate only one project.The functional form of the production function is:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y=xk^{\alpha}\left(\ell_{e}+\xi zn_{h}\right)^{\gamma}\ \ \mbox{where}\ \alpha,\gamma>0.\ \label{eq:firm_prod}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 so self-employment is feasible at 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}=0$
\end_inset

 ,
 while employer entrepreneurship corresponds to 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}>0$
\end_inset

 .
 Managerial talent 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is determined by the Pareto distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $F(x)=\left[1+\frac{x-\varsigma}{\xi}\right]^{-\vartheta_{i}}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta$
\end_inset

 is the shape parameter,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varsigma$
\end_inset

 the location parameter and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi$
\end_inset

 is the scale parameter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Factor remuneration:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Firms rent capital at the common market rate 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Government:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The government runs a balanced budget each period and provides unemployment insurance which is financed through lump sum taxation 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

,
 which is shared by the employee 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\psi)\tau$
\end_inset

 and employer 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi\tau$
\end_inset

.
 This program guarantees each household an unemployment benefit of 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Firm's problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The firm's problem is:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max_{n,k}Xk^{\alpha}n^{\gamma}-(1+\tau\psi)wzn-rk-\kappa\frac{\rho zn}{q(\theta_{t})}\label{eq:max_firm}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

employees must pay a share of unemployment insurance 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 to all workers who produce output.
 Firms have to pay search costs 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 for all vacancies posted.
 The number of vacancies posted is designed to replace any lost workers,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho zn.$
\end_inset

 This,
 however,
 is affected by the firm-hiring rate 
\begin_inset Formula $q(\theta_{t})=\bar{m}\left(\frac{v_{t}}{u_{t}}\right)^{-\mu}$
\end_inset

,
 where aggregate vacancies 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{t}$
\end_inset

 is simply the aggregate level of all those who have separate from firms 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum\rho zn$
\end_inset

 within the period and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the total unemployed looking for work within that period (see appendix).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the market is tight and the hiring rate is low,
 firms have to search more and thus face higher search costs in order to replace lost workers.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{This is because }0<q(\theta_{t})<1,$
\end_inset

so a lower 
\begin_inset Formula $q(\theta_{t})$
\end_inset

 increases 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\rho zn}{q(\theta_{t})}$
\end_inset

.
 If the firm lose 10 workers,
 they want to hire 10 workers to replace them.
 But if the hiring rate,
 
\begin_inset Formula $q(\theta_{t}),$
\end_inset

 is 50% then they will face double the amount of search costs than if 
\begin_inset Formula $q(\theta_{t})$
\end_inset

 were 100%.
 The upshot of this is that when markets are tight,
 the size of the desired firm size 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 decreases ex ante ,
 as the cost of replacing workers is high and so entrepreneurs would prefer to run smaller firms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To avoid issues of directed search,
 we assume that employees are randomly matched with firms (as in Chivers et al.,
 2015) and each firm employees a distribution of workers across the human capital spectrum such that each firm employs the market average.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Optimal behavior and equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In any time period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 agents are distinguished by 
\begin_inset Formula $(h,a,x,i,z)$
\end_inset

.
 The timing of the economy is given as follows.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Households enter each new period with assets 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and an employment status 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{w}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Idiosyncratic shocks 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is drawn which determines 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 (managerial ability) and 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 (labor productivity),
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 seperation rate conditional on education level,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P,S$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $T.$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Households make an occupation decision:
 entrepreneur (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{e}=1$
\end_inset

) or worker (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{e}=0$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Capital and labor markets clear.
 A fraction 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 of employees separate from firms and
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $p(\theta)$
\end_inset

 are hired to replace them 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Households choose:
 consumption (
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

),
 borrowing/saving (
\begin_inset Formula $a'$
\end_inset

) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Firm manager
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Firms are distinguished by their productivity realization 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 In the self-employment version,
 entrepreneurs choose capital and hired labor while also supplying owner labor to the business.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}$
\end_inset

 denote hired labor and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell_{e}$
\end_inset

 denote owner labor.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}=0$
\end_inset

 the entrepreneur is self-employed;
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}>0$
\end_inset

 the entrepreneur is an employer.
 The static problem for a manager with talent 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}$
\end_inset

 is  
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max_{k,n_{h}\geq0}xk^{\alpha}\left(\ell_{e}+\xi zn_{h}\right)^{\gamma}-zw(1+\psi\tau)n_{h}-rk-\kappa\frac{\rho zn_{h}}{q(\theta)}-f_{hire}\mathbf{1}\{n_{h}>0\}\label{maxP}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Self-employment is therefore the corner 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}=0$
\end_inset

 ,
 while employer entrepreneurship corresponds to 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}>0$
\end_inset

 .
 For an interior employer choice,
 the first-order condition for hired labor is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\gamma xk^{\alpha}\left(\ell_{e}+\xi zn_{h}\right)^{\gamma-1}\xi z=w(1+\psi\tau)+\frac{\rho\kappa}{q(\theta)}\label{maxP-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
n^{*}_{h}(k,x,\widetilde{w})=\max\left\{ 0,\frac{1}{\xi z}\left[\left(\frac{\gamma xk^{\alpha}\xi z}{w(1+\psi\tau)+\frac{\rho\kappa}{q(\theta)}}\right)^{\frac{1}{1-\gamma}}-\ell_{e}\right]\right\} \label{FOC}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FOC"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) into (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "maxP"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) yields the entrepreneur's profit function:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi(k,n^{*}_{h},x;\widetilde{w})=xk^{\alpha}\left(\ell_{e}+\xi zn^{*}_{h}\right)^{\gamma}-zn^{*}_{h}w(1+\psi\tau)-rk-\kappa\frac{\rho zn^{*}_{h}}{q(\theta)}-f_{hire}\mathbf{1}\{n^{*}_{h}>0\}\label{profitj}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Capital
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Initially we assume capital markets function perfectly,
 so the interior capital choice satisfies
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha xk^{\alpha-1}\left(\ell_{e}+\xi zn_{h}\right)^{\gamma}=r.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Entrepreneurs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Entrepreneurs maximize expected discounted utility of consumption 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{with }n_{h}=0\text{ denoting nonemployer self-employment and }n_{h}>0\text{ denoting employer entrepreneurship.}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\{c_{t},a_{t+1},k_{t},n_{h,t}\}}\mathbb{E}\sum^{\infty}_{t=0}\beta^{t}U(c_{t})
\]

\end_inset

subject to the following budget constraint:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
c+a' & \leq(1+r-\delta)a+\pi(k,n_{h},x;\tilde{r},w,\tau)-\tau_{\pi}\label{eq:c_worker-3}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a'\geq-\bar{a}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Employed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The workforce (previous periods employed who choose to reapply for work) maximize expected discounted utility of consumption 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\{c_{t},a_{t+1}\}}\mathbb{E}\sum^{\infty}_{t=0}\beta^{t}U(c_{t})
\]

\end_inset

subject to the following budget constraint:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
c+a' & \leq(1+r-\delta)a+(1-\rho)\left(wz(1-(1-\psi)\tau_{y})\right)+\rho b\label{eq:c_worker}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a'\geq-\bar{a}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 is the unemployment benefit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Unemployed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The unemployed maximize expected discounted utility of consumption 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\{c_{t},a_{t+1}\}}\mathbb{E}\sum^{\infty}_{t=0}\beta^{t}U(c_{t})
\]

\end_inset

subject to the following budget constraint:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
c+a' & \leq(1+r-\delta)a+p(\theta)\left(wz(1-(1-\psi)\tau_{y})\right)+(1-p(\theta))b\label{eq:c_worker-2}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a'\geq-\bar{a}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 is the unemployment benefit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Government 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The government runs a balanced budget with a lump-sum taxes 
\begin_inset Formula $T(y)=(y-\lambda_{p}y^{1-\tau_{p}})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The household's problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{w}$
\end_inset

 denote the household's labor market status in the previous period,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{w}=2$
\end_inset

 indicating being entrepreneur,
 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{w}=1$
\end_inset

 being employed and 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{w}=0$
\end_inset

 being unemployed.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{e}$
\end_inset

 indicate occupational choice,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{e}=1$
\end_inset

 if the household is an entrepreneur and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{e}=0$
\end_inset

 if the household is a worker.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}_{\rho(h)}$
\end_inset

 denote the continuation operator for employed workers who face job separation,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}_{p(\theta)}$
\end_inset

 denote the continuation operator for households searching for work,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}_{\rho_{e}(h)}$
\end_inset

 denote the continuation operator for entrepreneurs facing business closure.
 We write 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{V}_{0}$
\end_inset

 for the continuation value after business closure;
 in the benchmark this state does not automatically receive regular unemployment insurance.
 More specifically,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}_{p(\theta)}\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w}) & =p(\theta)\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w}=1)+(1-p(\theta))\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w}=0)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}_{\rho(h)}\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w}) & =(1-\rho(h))\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w}=1)+\rho(h)\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w}=0)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}_{\rho_{e}(h)}\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w}) & =(1-\rho_{e}(h))\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w}=2)+\rho_{e}(h)\mathbf{V}_{0}(a',h,x',i'_{w}=0)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{V}(a,h,x,i_{w}) & =\max\left\{ V^{w}(a,h,x,i_{w}),V^{e}(a,h,x)\right\} \\
V^{w}(a,h,x,i_{w}) & =\max_{\left\{ a',c\right\} }\left\{ u(c)+\iota_{i_{w}=1}\beta\mathbb{E}_{\rho(h)}\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w})+\iota_{i_{w}\neq1}\beta\mathbb{E}_{p(\theta)}\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w})\right\} \\
V^{e}(a,h,x) & =\max_{\left\{ a',c,k,n_{h}\geq0\right\} }\left\{ u(c)+\chi_{se}\mathbf{1}\{n_{h}=0\}+\chi_{emp}\mathbf{1}\{n_{h}>0\}+\beta\mathbb{E}_{\rho_{e}(h)}\mathbf{V}(a',h,x',i'_{w})\right\} 
\end{align*}

\end_inset

subject to
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
c+a' & \leq(1-\tilde{{r}}-\delta)a+inc\label{eq:c_worker-1}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
inc & =\begin{cases}
ra+\widetilde{w}_{n} & \mbox{ if \ensuremath{\mathcal{I}_{e}=0,} \ensuremath{i_{w}=1}}\\
ra+\widetilde{w}_{u} & \mbox{ if \ensuremath{\mathcal{I}_{e}=0,} \ensuremath{i_{w}\neq1}}\\
ra+\pi(k,n_{h},x;\tilde{r},w,\tau) & \mbox{ if \ensuremath{\mathcal{I}_{e}=1}}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $inc$
\end_inset

 is the earnings of the worker or entrepreneur,
 with entrepreneurial income coming from either self-employment (
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}=0$
\end_inset

 ) or employer entrepreneurship (
\begin_inset Formula $n_{h}>0$
\end_inset

 ),
 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{w}_{n}=(1-\rho)\left\{ zw(1-(1-\psi)\tau)\right\} +\rho b$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{w}_{u}=p(\theta)\left\{ zw(1-(1-\psi)\tau)\right\} +\{1-p(\theta)\}b$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the quantitative model discretizes human capital into three education groups,
 the operative occupational-choice object is a status-specific managerial-ability critical value within each human-capital block.
 For a household with assets 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset
,
human capital 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset
,
 and labor-market status 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset
 (employed or unemployed),
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{s}^{*}(a,h)$
\end_inset
 denote the minimum managerial ability required for entrepreneurship.
 Households choose entrepreneurship whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $x\geq x_{s}^{*}(a,h)$
\end_inset
.
 The value of retaining an existing wage match implies
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{\mathrm{unemp}}^{*}(a,h)\leq x_{\mathrm{emp}}^{*}(a,h).
\]

\end_inset

We define necessity entrepreneurship as households whose entrepreneurial ability is high enough to enter if unemployed but not high enough to enter if employed:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{\mathrm{unemp}}^{*}(a,h)\leq x<x_{\mathrm{emp}}^{*}(a,h).
\]

\end_inset

We define opportunity-driven entrepreneurship as households that enter regardless of labor-market status:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x\geq x_{\mathrm{emp}}^{*}(a,h)\geq x_{\mathrm{unemp}}^{*}(a,h).
\]

\end_inset

With three human-capital groups,
 the benchmark therefore yields six status-specific critical-value schedules at each asset level:
 employed and unemployed critical values for the low-,
 middle-,
 and high-human-capital blocks.
\end_layout

